WHY THIS POSITION, measured 2026-10-03 (writer spec §2):
  v_gex_strike_rank and v_gex_strike_walls READ volatility_snapshots
  (pg_get_viewdef probe: t for both; f for v_gex_concentration, v_gex_max_pain,
  v_gex_pin_maxpain, v_gex_repriced_flip). volatility_snapshots lands at the
  run_volatility step. So this is the FIRST instant at which every bound source
  sits on one clock. Hooking the writer at the end of
  compute_gamma_metrics_local.py would have read atm_iv_used, sigma,
  dist_sigma, band_used and both walls from the PREVIOUS cycle.

GRANULARITY, measured 2026-10-03 — why no once-per-cycle guard is needed:
  run_pipeline() is PER-SYMBOL (main() loops `for symbol in SYMBOLS`), and each
  call runs its own run_ingest(symbol), so EACH SYMBOL GETS ITS OWN run_id. A
  run_id carries exactly one (symbol, expiry_date) row in gamma_metrics -- 493
  of 493 run_ids since 09-29, zero carrying two symbols. So
  run_gex_cycle_history fires once per symbol with a DIFFERENT run_id each
  time, each writing only that symbol's legs. No double-write, no duplicated
  reads, and the two writer ExecutionLog rows per cycle are correct -- they
  mirror gamma and volatility, which also log once per symbol. No guard is
  required.

WHY IT DOES NOT ABORT THE PIPELINE:
  Unlike gamma and volatility, this step's failure is NOT a reason to skip
  run_market_state. gex_cycle_history has no live consumer until phase-2, while
  market_state feeds live surfaces — stopping the pipeline on a history-write
  failure would convert an inert gap into a display outage. That is the S67
  lesson restated: a downstream consumer's "not ready" must not terminate the
  upstream producer's progress. The step therefore logs and continues, and its
  own ExecutionLog row carries the failure.

Apply with:  git apply patches/2026-10-03_s89_orchestrator_gex_cycle_history_step.diff

